WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Temporary researchers created a vitamin B2 hydrogel that cross-links with blue light under cell-friendly conditions for tissue models. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 159. A K-A-I-S-T research team led by Professor Kwong-Hion Cho identified the causal circuits responsible for irreversibility in intracellular molecular networks. They developed a fundamental control technology called root that can regulate these circuits. The technology restores biological

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:59.900
states to their original conditions. This marks the first demonstration that irreversibility in intracellular networks can be controlled. The approach targets fundamental molecular decision points, rather than downstream effects. The work focuses on cells that have locked into abnormal states, such as those seen in cancer. Researchers will next test root in additional disease models. The Planetterrian spotlight on this work notes that the root method identifies the molecular circuits that lock cells into

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:25.280
irreversible abnormal states, and provides a control technology to reset them. By targeting the decision points that maintain cancer-like phenotypes, the approach moves beyond the symptom-focused interventions toward restoration of prior cellular identity. It directly addresses one of the central open questions in regenerative biology, whether cells that have crossed apparent points of no return can be returned to functional normalcy.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:48.440
The next data to watch are the first tests of root in intact tissue or animal models of disease, now shifting to a very different area of research. Researchers at Tampere University developed a versatile hydrogel platform that makes it easier to create customized biomaterials. Their plug-and-play cross-linking technology enables the design of a wide range of hydrogels.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:02:03.060
Biological molecules such as proteins, peptides, and nucleic acids can be incorporated under gentle cell-friendly conditions. The method relies on vitamin B2 and blue light to form the gels while preserving cell viability.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:13.940
The platform supports applications in tissue engineering, disease modeling, drug discovery, and regenerative medicine. The work appears in the journal Cell Reports Physical Science.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:24.560
Next steps include testing customized biomaterials in specific disease models. Building new tissues leads naturally to watching how natural tissues assemble in the first place.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:39.040
Johns Hopkins Medicine researchers developed a technology that captures a three-dimensional molecular snapshot of hundreds of hair follicles as they develop. They then reconstructed the fourth dimension of time to create a stop-motion animation.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:48.760
The animation reveals how this organ forms from initial cell clusters. The approach records dynamic spatial and temporal changes during organ development.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:57.260
The research was published in the journal Cell. The method provides a new way to observe how organs assemble in three dimensions over time.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:08.260
Future work will apply the same technique to other developing tissues. Observing how healthy organs form sets up questions about what goes wrong when cells are misidentified and removed.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:17.860
New findings indicate that in amiotrophic lateral sclerosis, microglia mistakes stressed but viable neurons for dead cells and engulf them.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:28.980
Altered signaling changes the recognition process that normally distinguishes living from dead neurons. The study suggests this mechanism may operate in other neurodegenerative conditions.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:40.780
It reframes microglial activity from passive cleanup to active contributor to neuronal loss. The work focuses on the early stages of disease when neurons remain metabolically active.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:52.380
Researchers examined surface markers that triggered the engulfment response. Most people picture microglia as the brain's cleanup crew that only removes dead or dying neurons after damage has occurred.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.880
In reality, these resident immune cells continuously survey living neurons and can engulf them when signaling goes awry.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:09.760
Right now, as you listen, microglia throughout your cortex are extending and retracting processes that contact neuronal surfaces and assess their metabolic state.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:20.760
In amiotrophic lateral sclerosis, stressed neurons display altered surface markers that microglia misread as death signals, leading them to consume still viable cells.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:30.200
One memorable figure is that microglia comprise roughly 10-15% of all cells in the adult human brain, yet perform this constant monitoring.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:39.560
The practical takeaway is that future therapies may need to correct the recognition step itself rather than simply reducing overall microglial activity.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:49.080
Watch for studies that measure surface marker changes on neurons in early neurodegenerative disease to see whether this mechanism appears in additional conditions.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:56.180
Cellular miscommunication inside the body parallels overlooked chemical processes that reshape entire ecosystems.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:03.140
A concordia study uncovered a previously overlooked chemical process that binds phosphorus in lake sediments.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:09.100
The mechanism prevents phosphorus from returning to the water column where it fuels plant and algal growth.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:16.200
The findings help explain why some lakes continue to experience algal blooms after external phosphorus inputs decline.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:21.520
The work points to internal sediment chemistry as a key control on nutrient recycling.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.920
Researchers identified the pathway beneath the surface of affected lakes.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.240
The results offer a new angle for managing eutrophication in freshwater systems.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.040
Hidden environmental chemistry gives way to hidden geological signals generated by everyday weather.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:45.520
Penn State researchers showed that seismic waves generated by thunderstorms can serve as a source for imaging near surface geological features.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:51.200
The method uses naturally occurring thunder-induced vibrations rather than artificial sources.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:55.760
It provides a low-cost way to map shallow structures across wide areas.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.240
The study demonstrates detection of subsurface interfaces using existing storm activity.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.880
Thunder quakes produce seismic waves that travel through the ground and reflect off underground layers.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.040
The approach relies on data collected during actual thunderstorms.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.720
Natural signals from storms connect to how plants use natural soil signals across generations.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:24.380
Research shows that plants under herbivore attack leave chemical signals that allow soil bacteria to form a memory of the event.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:30.560
Subsequent plants grown in that soil produce volatiles that draw wasps to attack the herbivores.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.400
The pathway creates an indirect defense that operates across plant generations.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.600
The mechanism adds a microbial layer to plant herbivore wasp interactions.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:45.100
Bacteria in the soil retain the chemical record after the initial attack.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.120
New plants then respond by emitting the wasp attracting compounds.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.740
From surface ecosystems we drop to conditions deep inside the planet.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:04.480
Science Tokyo experiments using laser-heated diamond anvil cells produced signatures of super-ionic iron hydride at pressures and temperatures matching Earth's inner core.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:10.000
The results suggest light elements such as hydrogen can create this exotic state in the core.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:14.240
The work supplies new constraints on core composition and dynamics.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.220
Researchers identified experimental signatures of the super-ionic phase under relevant conditions.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.800
The findings provide new insights into the composition and dynamics of Earth's deep interior.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.420
Further measurements will refine models of core heat flow.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:35.380
Before we go, keep an eye on the first tests of the root technology in animal models of disease.

